Day 44/100 of #DrGVishwanathanChallenge
Today's learning was focused on Binary Trees and understanding Depth-First Search (DFS) using recursion.
 Problem 1: 94. Binary Tree Inorder Traversal (Easy)
 Approach:
Used recursive DFS.
Followed the Left → Root → Right (LNR) traversal order.
Visited the left subtree first, then the current node, and finally the right subtree.
Stored each visited node in the result list.

 Time Complexity:O(n) – Every node is visited exactly once.
Space Complexity :O(h) – Recursive call stack, where h is the height of the tree.

Problem 2: 144. Binary Tree Preorder Traversal (Easy)
 Approach:
Used recursive DFS.
Followed the Root → Left → Right (NLR) traversal order.
Visited the current node first, then recursively traversed the left and right subtrees.
Collected all node values in the required order.

Time Complexity: O(n) – Every node is visited once.
Space Complexity:O(h) – Recursive stack space, where h is the height of the tree.

Key Learnings:
1) Understood the difference between Inorder and Preorder traversals.
2) Strengthened recursive thinking for tree-based problems.
3) Learned how changing the order of recursive calls changes the traversal.
